    pr_ref:  # 9956
    description: "Found 2026-11-14 while checking, at the owner's request, whether coverage narrowing could be applied per changed file. Evidence and scripts: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/per_file_narrowing_check_11142026 (README.md).\n\nOBSERVED. The selection index (~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/selection_index.sqlite) no longer holds edges it held on 2026-11-12, for tests that still execute the code. Compared with the format-1 backup written 11-12 23:27 (selection_index.sqlite.format-1.zst), main checkout only, counting test-to-source-file pairs where the test still has edges and both files still exist:\n- 26,212 pairs lost, on 7,497 tests in 574 test files (412,614 pairs then, 392,265 now).\n- tests in hypergumbo-lang-mainstream to source in hypergumbo-lang-mainstream: 17,036 of 18,037 lost.\n- tests in hypergumbo-core to source in hypergumbo-lang-mainstream: 5,939 of 7,402 lost.\n- tests in hypergumbo-lang-common to source in hypergumbo-lang-common: 1,149 of 1,244 lost.\nDIRECT CHECK. packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_an_import_is_not_a_call.py, run alone on 11-14 with every package measured: 8 of its coverage contexts hold lines of hypergumbo_lang_mainstream/py.py. The index holds no edge from any of its tests to py.py. On 11-12 two of its tests had 101 and 165 edges into hypergumbo-lang-mainstream; today all of their edges (1,205 and 1,181) are in hypergumbo-core.\n\nMECHANISM (read in the code).\n1. selection_index.store_edges deletes EVERY edge of each test named in a run, then inserts the edges that run produced (DELETE FROM test_block WHERE test = ?). The module docstring's rule 1 (replace, do not union, per test) guards against a targeted run erasing tests that did not run. It does not guard against a run that measured only part of the source.\n2. A targeted smart-test run measures only the packages that hold a changed source file (build_affected_cov_paths: one --cov per package directory). After a change confined to hypergumbo-core, every test that ran keeps only its edges into hypergumbo-core; its edges into every other package are deleted, though that run could not have seen them either way.\n3. The test stays speakable (it has rows and is not unmeasured), so narrowing may drop it, and the coverage union can no longer add it.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, SIMULATED ON TODAY'S INDEX. A change to function bodies only in py.py (nothing refuses): the import slice selects 267 test files; narrowing drops 183; 66 of the 183 were recorded executing py.py on 11-12 (the file above is one). With the 11-12 index the same narrowing drops 114 and none of the 66. Narrowing is on by default and narrows the manifest merge CI runs (owner ruling 2026-11-12), so those 66 files would not run before merge. The local whole-file coverage gate does not catch it when the kept tests still cover every line. The full-suite cron does.\n\nALSO SEEN, NOT TRACED.\n- Test ids are stored repo-relative and source paths absolute, so one test id is shared by every checkout while its edges name one checkout's files. The file table holds more than 40 worktree copies of py.py. 120 tests have edges and none to a main-checkout file. A run in a worktree replacing a test's main-checkout edges is the same defect by another route; not reproduced.\n- The test files added on the 11-14 branch (test_readme_prose.py and others) and the new source file readme_prose.py are absent from the index although they ran many times through smart-test with explicit test paths. Whether such runs are meant to fold into the index was not checked.\n\nASK.\n(a) Replacement must be limited to what the run measured: delete a test's edges only for source files inside that run's coverage scope, and keep the rest. The scope has to come from the run (its --cov roots or coverage's own measured-file list), not from which files happened to produce rows.\n(b) Decide how edges are keyed across checkouts (repo-relative source paths, or one index per checkout).\n(c) A test that fails today: fold a run that measured every package, then a run of the same tests that measured one package, and assert the edges into the other packages survive. Add the worktree case once (b) is decided.\n(d) After the fix, the lost edges come back only when the tests run again with their sources measured: a full local run, or a restore from the 11-12 backup followed by one. Say which in the PR.\n(e) Re-run the py.py simulation above (scripts in the evidence directory) and show 0 of the dropped files were recorded executing py.py.\n\nGENERALITY. The rule is about any run whose coverage scope is narrower than the code a test executes, in any package and any checkout. A fix keyed to hypergumbo-core and hypergumbo-lang-mainstream does not close this.\n\nNOT VERIFIED. Which runs between 11-12 and 11-14 removed which edges (the mechanism was read in the code and matches the per-package pattern of the loss; no single run was replayed). How many PRs have merged with a test wrongly dropped: the manifest header shows narrowing removed tests on 2 of the 88 PRs since 10-30 that changed package source; neither was examined."  # 9956
    fields:  # 9956
      statement: "An edge from a test to a source file stays in the selection index until a run that measured that source file shows the test no longer executes it."  # 9956
      root_cause: "selection_index.store_edges deletes every edge of each test in a run, while a targeted smart-test run measures only the packages that hold a changed source file; test ids are repo-relative and source paths absolute, so checkouts share test ids."  # 9956
